/**
 * Print 1 to N and Print N to 1
 * Topic: Recursion (Linear) — Call Order vs Return Order
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS:
 * - n >= 0
 * - No loops allowed — pure recursion
 *
 * KEY INSIGHT:
 * - Print BEFORE recursive call → executes during call phase → N to 1
 * - Print AFTER recursive call → executes during return phase → 1 to N
 * - This exact concept appears in tree traversals (pre/in/post order)
 *
 * =====================================================================
 * APPROACH — Print N to 1
 * =====================================================================
 * HOW: Print n first, then recurse into n-1.
 *      During call phase: 5 → 4 → 3 → 2 → 1 → base case
 *      Output: 5 4 3 2 1
 *
 * TC: O(n) | SC: O(n) — n stack frames
 */
public static void printNto1(int n) {
    if (n == 0) return;        // base case
    System.out.println(n);     // print BEFORE call → call phase
    printNto1(n - 1);          // recurse into smaller problem
}

/**
 * =====================================================================
 * APPROACH — Print 1 to N
 * =====================================================================
 * HOW: Recurse first, print after returning.
 *      During return phase: base → 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5
 *      Output: 1 2 3 4 5
 *
 * COMMON MISTAKE: Printing before the call thinking it will print in order.
 *      Printing before call always gives reverse order (N to 1).
 *      To get ascending order, print must happen on the WAY BACK UP.
 *
 * TC: O(n) | SC: O(n) — n stack frames
 */
public static void print1toN(int n) {
    if (n == 0) return;        // base case
    print1toN(n - 1);          // recurse first
    System.out.println(n);     // print AFTER call → return phase
}
